Generate Certificates for SAML Encryption
Since we are using pre-shared keys, generate the required key pairs.

📌 Generate the SP Key Pair
Run the following command to create a private key and certificate for the SP:

bash
Copy
Edit
openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -keyout sp_private.key -out sp_cert.pem -days 365 -nodes
Convert the private key to PKCS8 format for Java:

bash
Copy
Edit
openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -inform PEM -outform PEM -nocrypt -in sp_private.key -out sp_private_pkcs8.key
Now, you have:

sp_private.key → SP private key
sp_cert.pem → SP public certificate (used by the IdP for assertion encryption)
2️⃣ Set Up PingFederate as the IdP
Login to PingFederate Admin Console

URL: https://localhost:9999/pingfederate
Admin Credentials:
Username: Administrator
Password: <your_password>
Go to "Federation" → "IdP Configuration"

Create a new SP Connection

Name: SpringBootSP
Manually configure the connection (No metadata needed)
Assertion Encryption Settings:
Select Encrypt Assertions
Upload sp_cert.pem as the SP's encryption certificate
Configure SSO URL

Assertion Consumer Service (ACS) URL:
bash
Copy
Edit
http://localhost:8080/login/saml2/sso
This is where the SP receives the SAML response.
Enable the IdP-Initiated SSO URL

bash
Copy
Edit
https://localhost:9999/idp/startSSO.ping?PartnerSpId=SpringBootSP
3️⃣ Create Spring Boot SAML2 SP
We will create a Spring Boot 3 application that acts as a SAML2 SP.

📌 Add Dependencies (Maven)
xml
Copy
Edit
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-security-saml2-service-provider</artifactId>
</dependency>
📌 Configure application.yml
yaml
Copy
Edit
server:
  port: 8080

spring:
  security:
    saml2:
      relyingparty:
        registration:
          my-sp:
            entity-id: "http://localhost:8080/saml2/sp"
            assertingparty:
              metadata-uri: "https://localhost:9999/pf/federation/metadata/idp"
            decryption:
              credentials:
                - private-key-location: "classpath:sp_private_pkcs8.key"
                  certificate-location: "classpath:sp_cert.pem"
            signing:
              credentials:
                - private-key-location: "classpath:sp_private_pkcs8.key"
                  certificate-location: "classpath:sp_cert.pem"
📌 Configure Spring Security
Create SecurityConfig.java:

java
Copy
Edit
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.saml2.provider.service.registration.RelyingPartyRegistrationRepository;

@Configuration
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http, 
            RelyingPartyRegistrationRepository relyingPartyRegistrationRepository) throws Exception {
        
        http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth.anyRequest().authenticated())
            .saml2Login(saml2 -> saml2.defaultSuccessUrl("/home"));

        return http.build();
    }
}
📌 Create a Simple Controller
java
Copy
Edit
import org.springframework.security.core.annotation.AuthenticationPrincipal;
import org.springframework.security.saml2.provider.service.authentication.Saml2AuthenticatedPrincipal;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
public class HomeController {

    @GetMapping("/home")
    public String home(@AuthenticationPrincipal Saml2AuthenticatedPrincipal principal) {
        return "Hello " + principal.getName() + "! Your email is: " + principal.getAttribute("email");
    }
}
4️⃣ Start and Test the SAML Flow
🔹 Start the Spring Boot SP
bash
Copy
Edit
mvn spring-boot:run
🔹 Perform SSO
Open PingFederate IdP SSO URL:
bash
Copy
Edit
https://localhost:9999/idp/startSSO.ping?PartnerSpId=SpringBootSP
Login with your test credentials.
You will be redirected to http://localhost:8080/home with your SAML attributes.
📌 Verifying Encrypted Assertions
To manually check the encrypted assertion:

Capture the SAML Response

Open DevTools (F12) → Go to Network → Find the POST request to /login/saml2/sso
Copy the SAMLResponse (Base64 encoded).
Decode it

bash
Copy
Edit
echo "BASE64_SAML_RESPONSE" | base64 -d > saml_response.xml
Extract Encrypted Assertion

bash
Copy
Edit
xmllint --format saml_response.xml | grep "<EncryptedAssertion>"
🎯 Summary
✅ PingFederate acts as the IdP
✅ Spring Boot handles SAML authentication
✅ Assertions are encrypted using SP’s public key
✅ SP decrypts the assertion using its private key
✅ User is authenticated and redirected to /home

